# Abstract

As an engineering organisation, we should write specifications for the work we do, and we should do it in a consistent and orderly manner. Specifications are used to organise and document the process of software development. This meta-specification describes the usage of specifications within the Engineering organisation.

# Rationale

The purpose of creating engineering specifications is to ensure careful consideration of the problem and proposed solution before moving forward with implementation. By writing a spec, the team is encouraged to think thoroughly about the problem at hand and develop a well-thought-out solution. This process also promotes a shared understanding of the problem and the proposed solution among team members. The written spec facilitates peer review, iteration, and ultimately leads to an agreed-upon approach. Additionally, having specs provides cross-team visibility, as the work of one team may impact or interest other teams. It is crucial for other teams to be aware of and able to provide feedback on the design of work conducted by another team. Lastly, specs serve as a record of the work that has been done.

It is important to note that specs do not need to be excessively detailed. Even concise specs can provide the aforementioned benefits.

## Goals

Our approach to engineering specifications aims to achieve the following goals:

1. **Active Discoverability**: Interested parties and stakeholders should be able to easily locate the spec documents for the features or topics they are interested in. Spec documents should be readily accessible without the need for direct communication. To accomplish this, we need to establish a [centralised repository or index of specs](https://specs.canonical.com/) that encompasses the entire engineering organisation.  
2. **Consensus**: One of the main advantages of having specs is to foster a shared understanding and agreement on proposals. Peers should have the opportunity to thoroughly comprehend the proposal, ask questions, provide comments for clarification, and suggest improvements. Iteration should take place until a common consensus is reached. To facilitate this collaborative process, we will use Google Docs, which provides helpful tools for commenting, suggesting changes, and group editing.  
3. **Consistency**: All teams are expected to create specs for their projects. If a feature is included in the roadmap, it must have a corresponding spec. Even if a feature is not on the roadmap, it may still require a spec. Specs should be developed prior to implementation, although prototyping and experimentation are not excluded during the design and spec-writing phase. Before approval, the author's manager and peers should review the spec. Specs should follow a consistent naming convention and be stored in a centralised location or index.

