## Approval Process

### List of approvers

The list of approvers includes people who will benefit from implementing the specification or are involved in its development. It's the responsibility of the specification author to use their best judgement and ask for input from all relevant parties. The author or their manager or team lead may pre-fill rows in the spec history with approvers they feel should review and approve the spec.

### How to approve a spec

The author of the specification defines the list of reviewers and adds it to the metadata table.  
![][image4]  
A reviewer can approve the spec by updating the ***metadata table*** and adding a new row in the ***Spec History and Changelog*** table with the current date, “***Approved***” status, and their name. This helps track changes since the last approval.  
Once all the main stakeholders approved the specification, the status of the specification could be also transitioned to “***Approved***” status by the specification author.

Expected outcome of a specification, such as software implementation effort, must not start until the related specification has been approved.

***Reviewers are responsible for providing timely review.***

## Cross-team Specs

In the event that multiple teams are contributing to a single spec, the spec should reside in the folder of the team that is bearing the most responsibility and ownership of the work. Second team may create a Google Drive shortcut to the specification in their team folder.

